WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.400
Hello, everybody.
大家好。

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:08.460
Today, we are rerunning the third episode in Season 5, Business Ethics.
今天我们要重播第五季的第三集《商业道德》。

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.440
This is the episode where Michael and Holly have lunch at Cooper's Seafood.
这一集里迈克尔和霍莉在库珀海鲜餐厅吃了午饭。

4
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.580
Yes, and we went into our mailbag and found this fun catch from Seth H.
没错，我们翻了翻读者的来信，发现了塞思·H发来的这个有趣的发现。

5
00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.740
and several others.
还有其他几位观众也提到了。

6
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.660
Seth wrote in and said this,
塞思写道：

7
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.400
I have a continuity catch from this episode.
我发现这一集有个剧情连贯性的漏洞。

8
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.300
At Cooper's, Holly says she's from Des Moines.
在库珀餐厅，霍莉说她来自得梅因。

9
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:34.440
But when she and Michael get engaged in a later season, she needs to move, quote, home to Boulder, Colorado.
但当她和迈克尔在后几季订婚时，她需要搬回所谓的“老家”科罗拉多州博尔德。

10
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.700
So where is Holly really from?
所以霍莉到底来自哪里？

11
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:43.380
Jenna, I was curious about this, and I wondered if the show Bible said anything more about where Holly is from.
珍娜，我对此很好奇，我想知道剧集圣经里有没有关于霍莉家乡的更多说明。

12
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.360
Well, you know I love any chance to get out the show Bible.
你知道我一有机会就喜欢翻出剧集圣经。

13
00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.120
I know you do.
我知道你喜欢。

14
00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:53.140
So I checked it, and it said Holly is, quote, originally from Des Moines.
所以我查了一下，上面写着霍莉“最初来自得梅因”。

15
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.220
There is nothing about Colorado.
完全没提到科罗拉多州。

16
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:56.980
In the whole show Bible for Holly?
整本霍莉的剧集圣经里都没有吗？

17
00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.080
That's correct.
没错。

18
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:02.720
However, the show Bible stops at the end of Season 5.
不过，这本剧集圣经在第五季结束时就停止更新了。

19
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:03.680
Really?
真的吗？

20
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:04.280
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

21
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:10.740
Now, this was the show Bible that they gave me in Season 9 when I became a producer.
这是我在第九季成为制片人时他们给我的那本剧集圣经。

22
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:17.660
So they clearly would still reference it, but it had not been updated since the end of Season 5.
所以他们显然还会参考它，但它自第五季结束后就没再更新过。

23
00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:23.160
And I was wondering, could this be why there are so many continuity issues in later seasons?
我在想，这会不会就是后期剧集出现那么多连贯性问题的原因？

24
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:24.100
Yeah.
很有可能。

25
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.020
No one was updating the show Bible.
没人更新剧集圣经了。

26
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:26.680
Not anymore.
不再更新了。

27
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:34.020
Now, I do want to say that where you are, quote, from or what you consider your hometown can be two different places.
不过我想说，你“来自”哪里或者你认为哪里是你的家乡，这可能是两个不同的地方。

28
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:34.840
That's true.
确实如此。

29
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:39.660
For me, I was born in Fort Wayne, Indiana, but I grew up in St. Louis, Missouri.
对我来说，我出生在印第安纳州的韦恩堡，但我是在密苏里州的圣路易斯长大的。

30
00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.380
So I would probably say that I'm from St. Louis.
所以我可能会说我来自圣路易斯。

31
00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:44.860
I get that.
我明白你的意思。

32
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:53.820
I consider St. Louis my hometown, but it could still be accurate for me to say, oh, I'm from Fort Wayne, Indiana, but my hometown was St. Louis.
我把圣路易斯视为我的家乡，但如果我说“我来自印第安纳州韦恩堡，但我的家乡是圣路易斯”，这依然是准确的。

33
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:54.300
I don't know.
我不知道。

34
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.500
So maybe Holly was born in St. Louis, but she grew up in St. Louis, but she grew up in St. Louis.
所以也许霍莉出生在得梅因，但她在圣路易斯长大，或者诸如此类的情况。

35
00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.040
I don't know.
我不知道。

36
00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.100
I get it.
我懂了。

37
00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:08.140
When people ask me where you're from, I sometimes am perplexed because I'm like, well, I was born in Lafayette, Louisiana, but then I lived 12 years from 2 to 14 in Jakarta,
当人们问我来自哪里时，我有时会感到困惑，因为我会想，我出生在路易斯安那州的拉斐特，但从2岁到14岁，我在雅加达住了12年，

38
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:08.600
Indonesia.
也就是印度尼西亚。

39
00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:14.360
Then we moved to Texas, and my parents are from there, but then I moved to New York.
后来我们搬到了德克萨斯州，我父母是那里人，但后来我搬到了纽约。

40
00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.460
Then I moved to Los Angeles.
然后我又搬到了洛杉矶。

41
00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:16.160
I don't know.
我也不知道。

42
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:16.840
Yeah.
是啊。

43
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.580
I mean, I've now lived in L.A.
我的意思是，我现在在洛杉矶住的时间，

44
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.280
longer than I ever lived in St. Louis, so it's like, what is my hometown?
比我在圣路易斯住的时间还要长，所以我在想，哪里才是我的家乡？

45
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.860
Is this my hometown now?
现在这里是我的家乡吗？

46
00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:24.420
I don't know.
我不知道。

47
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:25.040
I don't know.
我不知道。

48
00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.320
I still think of it as St. Louis.
我还是觉得圣路易斯是我的家乡。

49
00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.840
I know, and then I still say when I go to see, you know, my mom, I'm going home.
我知道，而且当我去看我妈妈时，我还是会说“我要回家了”。

50
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:31.460
Right.
没错。

51
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:31.940
Right.
好的。

52
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:32.560
Yeah.
没错。

53
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:33.400
All right.
行吧。

54
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:40.780
Well, before we get to our rerun of Business Ethics, a little Office Ladies update to say that we have been working on our upcoming episodes.
好了，在重温《商业道德》这一集之前，先向大家同步一下《办公室女士》的近况，我们一直在制作接下来的节目。

55
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:49.000
We are super excited to announce that in addition to continuing the rewatch, we also have an interview with Catherine Tate coming up.
我们非常激动地宣布，除了继续重温剧集，我们还即将播出对凯瑟琳·塔特的采访。

56
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.080
It was so good.
太棒了。

57
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:57.520
She was in studio with us, and it is funny, and she is delightful, and she had so many memories.
她来到录音室和我们面对面交流，非常有趣，人也特别可爱，还分享了许多回忆。

58
00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.940
We can't wait for you to hear it.
迫不及待想让你们听听了。

59
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:00.200
Oh, I loved her.
噢，我太喜欢她了。

60
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:00.940
All right.
好了。

61
00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:06.960
Now, our rerun of Business Ethics, which includes an interview with another amazing woman, Amy Ryan.
现在，重温《商业道德》，其中还包括对另一位了不起的女性——艾米·瑞恩的采访。

62
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:07.960
Enjoy.
请欣赏。

63
00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:13.590
I'm Jenna Fisher.
我是珍娜·费舍尔。

64
00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:14.890
And I'm Angela Kinsey.
我是安吉拉·金赛。

65
00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:16.170
We were on The Office together.
我们曾一起出演《办公室》。

66
00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:17.410
And we're best friends.
我们是最好的朋友。

67
00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.250
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office Rewatch Podcast just for you.
现在，我们专为你们制作了《办公室》终极重温播客。

68
00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:28.230
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.
每周，我们都会深度解析一集《办公室》，并分享只有身临其境的我们才能告诉你的独家幕后故事。

69
00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:29.370
We're The Office Ladies.
我们是《办公室女士》。

70
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:34.340
Hi, everyone.
大家好。

71
00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:35.480
Hey, guys.
嘿，伙计们。

72
00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:36.600
What's up, Ange?
怎么了，安吉？

73
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.700
What's up with you and your cute pants?
你和你那条可爱的裤子是怎么回事？

74
00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:40.380
Thank you for saying that.
谢谢你的夸奖。

75
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:41.760
I noticed them right away.
我一眼就注意到了。

76
00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:42.680
Here's the thing.
是这样的。

77
00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:44.720
I hit a wall with sweatpants.
我穿运动裤穿腻了。

78
00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:45.740
What?
什么？

79
00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:46.460
I know.
我知道。

80
00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.540
Guys, one of the reasons we started this podcast was because I wanted a job I could do in sweatpants.
伙计们，我们做这个播客的原因之一，就是我想找一份能穿着运动裤完成的工作。

81
00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:51.940
Uh-huh.
嗯哼。

82
00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.280
You know, we talked about that a lot early on.
你知道，我们早期经常聊这个。

83
00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:54.600
Yeah.
没错。

84
00:03:54.600 --> 00:04:00.760
Well, I counted after 409 days of wearing sweatpants.
我算了一下，我已经穿了409天的运动裤了。

85
00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:09.180
I mean, not every single day, but for the most part, for the last 409 days, I would get out of bed and put on sweatpants.
我是说，虽然不是每一天，但大部分时间，在过去的409天里，我起床后穿的都是运动裤。

86
00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:09.520
Right.
也是。

87
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.700
I actually had several pairs of pants that were made of the same material as my sheets.
我其实有几条裤子，材质和我床单是一样的。

88
00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:16.520
Like a jersey?
像针织面料吗？

89
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.060
They're bamboo.
是竹纤维的。

90
00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:18.800
Okay.
好吧。

91
00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:21.560
Made by the same company that makes my sheets.
和做我床单的是同一家公司生产的。

92
00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:25.160
Listen, if I could have pants made by people that make bedding.
听着，如果我能穿上做床上用品的人做的裤子……

93
00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.640
That's what I thought.
我当时就是这么想的。

94
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.340
It's like I'm just wearing my bed around all day.
感觉就像整天把床穿在身上一样。

95
00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.540
I thought I'd hit the jackpot.
我觉得我中大奖了。

96
00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:38.540
Okay, so after 409 days of this, I hit a wall, and I was like, I need to wear pants, actual pants.
好了，在这样过了409天之后，我受够了，我想我得穿裤子，真正的裤子。

97
00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.560
So I went out, and I bought a pair of pants, Angela.
所以我出门买了一条裤子，安吉拉。

98
00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:42.260
I'm going to stand up.
我要站起来了。

99
00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:42.940
I'm going to show you.
我要展示给你看。

100
00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:44.100
I'm going to describe them, too.
我也会描述一下它们的样子。

101
00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.000
I'm going to take a picture.
我要拍张照。

102
00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:50.360
They kind of look like camouflage, but like very sassy cargo kind of pants.
它们看起来有点像迷彩裤，但更像是那种非常时髦的工装裤。

103
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.060
Yeah, that's what I was going for.
没错，我追求的就是这种效果。

104
00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:53.520
Sassy cargo?
时髦工装裤？

105
00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.160
Yeah, I love a sassy cargo.
对，我超爱这种时髦工装裤。

106
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.700
All right, so here's the thing.
好吧，事情是这样的。

107
00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.340
I think I'm so used to wearing sweatpants.
我想我太习惯穿运动裤了。

108
00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.180
I'm in the dressing room, and I don't know which size of pants to buy.
我现在在试衣间里，不知道该买哪个尺码的裤子。

109
00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:05.220
Because you've been wearing the baggy britches.
因为你一直穿着宽松的裤子。

110
00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:06.040
That's right.
没错。

111
00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:10.060
I went back and forth and back and forth, and I went with the bigger size.
我纠结来纠结去，最后选了大一码的。

112
00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:13.460
And now I've been wearing them for what?
结果现在我都穿了多久了？

113
00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:14.740
I don't know, an hour?
不知道，有一个小时了吧？

114
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.620
Now they're all stretched out.
现在裤子全撑大了。

115
00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:17.420
They're too big.
太大了。

116
00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:18.300
And look what happens.
你看，出问题了。

117
00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.700
Now they're falling down, and I've got long crotch.
现在裤子往下掉，裤裆都垂到大腿了。

118
00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:23.060
Long crotch, lady.
裤裆垂到大腿了，女士。

119
00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:23.540
Oh, I see it.
哦，我看到了。

120
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:24.660
You have long crotch.
你的裤裆确实垂得很低。

121
00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.460
Gals, you know what we're talking about.
姑娘们，你们懂我们在说什么。

122
00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:27.640
What do I do?
我该怎么办？

123
00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:32.460
When the seam of your pants is halfway between your thigh and your knees, that's long crotch.
当裤子的接缝处垂到大腿和膝盖中间时，那就是裤裆垂到大腿了。

124
00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:33.520
So what do I do?
那我该怎么办？

125
00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:34.300
I've worn them.
我已经穿过了。

126
00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:36.860
I can't take them back and get the smaller size.
我没法退货换小一码的了。

127
00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:39.680
I think I could get someone to alter it in the back.
我想我可以找人把后面改小一点。

128
00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:41.680
Oh, my mom could do that in two seconds.
哦，我妈两秒钟就能搞定。

129
00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:42.020
I know.
我知道。

130
00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:45.560
And the thing is, is I just, I'm trying to reenter.
问题是，我只是，我正在尝试重新融入社会。

131
00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.560
You're trying to reenter the world.
你正在尝试重新融入这个世界。

132
00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.740
You know, I like the sassy cargo minus the long crotch.
你知道，我喜欢这种时髦工装裤，只要不垂裆就行。

133
00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:52.500
What are we doing today?
我们今天要做什么？

134
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.200
All right, let me tell you.
好吧，让我来告诉你。

135
00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.740
For those of you guys wanting to know what we're doing today.
对于那些想知道我们今天要做什么的朋友们。

136
00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:01.080
It is Business Ethics, Season 5, Episode 3.
我们要讲的是《办公室》第五季第三集《商业道德》。

137
00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:01.980
Oh, yeah.
哦，太棒了。

138
00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:02.660
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

139
00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:06.040
It is written by Ryan Coe and directed by Jeffrey Blitz.
这一集由瑞安·科编剧，杰弗里·布里茨执导。

140
00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:07.400
Love it.
超爱这一集。

141
00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:08.600
Here is a summary.
这是剧情梗概。

142
00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.760
Jim reluctantly reveals him and Pam's engagement to the office.
吉姆不情愿地向办公室公开了他和帕姆订婚的消息。

143
00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:14.560
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

144
00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:14.880
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

145
00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:15.680
We'll discuss.
我们会讨论一下。

146
00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:23.140
Following Ryan's recent actions, corporate has required Holly to conduct a seminar on business
继瑞安最近的行为之后，公司要求霍莉举办一场关于商业道德的研讨会。

147
00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:23.700
ethics.
道德。

148
00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:29.320
Michael offers immunity to anyone willing to confess to dishonesty at work.
迈克尔向任何愿意承认在工作中不诚实行为的人提供豁免权。

149
00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.460
What are we, like an episode of Survivor?
我们这是在演《幸存者》吗？

150
00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:31.860
I know.
我知道。

151
00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:32.780
You get immunity.
你获得了豁免权。

152
00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.160
Everything you say.
你说的每一句话。

153
00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:36.140
And then we're going to light the torch for you.
然后我们要为你点燃火炬。

154
00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:38.140
Well, some things are revealed.
嗯，有些事情会被揭露出来。

155
00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:40.120
Again, we will discuss.
再说一次，我们会讨论的。

156
00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:43.980
And it's going to cause a rift between Michael and Holly.
这会在迈克尔和霍莉之间造成裂痕。

157
00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:44.540
Yeah.
没错。

158
00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:51.500
Meanwhile, Jim makes Dwight observe the company's time theft policy, and Meredith provides Outback
与此同时，吉姆让德怀特遵守公司的工时窃取政策，而梅雷迪思为整个办公室提供了澳拜客

159
00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.120
Steakhouse takeout to the entire office.
牛排馆的外卖。

160
00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:55.980
So that's our summary.
这就是我们的摘要。

161
00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.600
Would you like fast fact number one?
你想听第一个速记事实吗？

162
00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:00.420
I really would.
我真的很想听。

163
00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:01.200
All right.
好的。

164
00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:02.580
New writer alert.
新编剧预警。

165
00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:03.240
Oh.
哦。

166
00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:06.460
Writer Ryan Coe has joined the staff for Season 5.
编剧瑞安·科加入了第五季的制作团队。

167
00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:07.160
Hello, Ryan.
你好，瑞安。

168
00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:07.560
Welcome.
欢迎。

169
00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:08.120
Hello, Ryan.
你好，瑞安。

170
00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:12.600
He was only with us for one season as he left to work on Cougar Town.
他只和我们合作了一季，因为他后来去参与《熟女镇》的工作了。

171
00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:14.960
Oh, we knew lots of folks on Cougar Town.
哦，我们认识很多《熟女镇》的人。

172
00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:16.260
We sure did, Busy Phillips.
我们确实认识，比茜·菲利普斯。

173
00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:16.840
Oh, yeah.
哦，是的。

174
00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.520
He is also a writer and co-executive producer of The Simpsons.
他同时也是《辛普森一家》的编剧和联合执行制片人。

175
00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:21.560
Welcome, Ryan.
欢迎，瑞安。

176
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:22.320
Welcome.
欢迎。

177
00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.020
Fast fact number two.
第二个速记事实。

178
00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:25.540
No Pam.
没有帕姆。

179
00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:31.440
Outside of the cold open, where you hear Pam's voice, there is no Pam in this episode.
除了片头冷笑话里能听到帕姆的声音外，这一集里没有帕姆。

180
00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:32.940
But there was Pam.
但其实有帕姆。

181
00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:34.700
There was, you guys.
确实有，伙计们。

182
00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:36.300
There was.
确实有。

183
00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:40.100
Because I filmed a bunch of scenes at art school, and they all got cut out.
因为我在艺术学校拍了很多场景，但都被剪掉了。

184
00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:40.740
I know.
我知道。

185
00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:43.040
I saw in the deleted scenes, there's like three.
我在删减片段里看到了，大概有三个。

186
00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:46.520
Well, I'm going to talk about them as we break down this episode.
好吧，我会在分析这一集的时候谈到它们。

187
00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:50.540
On Office Ladies, Pam will be inserted into this episode again.
在《办公室女士》节目中，帕姆会再次被加入到这一集中。

188
00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:51.940
She will live again.
她将重获新生。

189
00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:52.980
She will.
她会的。

190
00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:58.480
And also, Jenna, there was an amazing Ryan Kevin runner that got deleted for time.
而且，珍娜，还有一个很棒的瑞安和凯文的连贯剧情因为时长被删掉了。

191
00:07:58.500 --> 00:07:59.240
And it's great.
而且非常棒。

192
00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:00.280
And I'm going to bring that up.
我会提出来的。

193
00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:04.660
You guys, we're bringing it all back today in business ethics.
伙计们，今天在《商业道德》这一集中，我们要把一切都找回来。

194
00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:08.420
Fast fact number three, Amy Ryan.
第三个速记事实，艾米·瑞安。

195
00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:10.540
Holly is here today.
霍莉今天来了。

196
00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:16.020
We interviewed Amy Ryan all about this episode and her time on The Office.
我们采访了艾米·瑞安，聊了聊这一集以及她在《办公室》的时光。

197
00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:17.400
Let's hear it.
让我们听听吧。

198
00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.280
Amy Ryan, welcome to Office Ladies.
艾米·瑞安，欢迎来到《办公室女士》。

199
00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:26.020
Hi, Office Ladies.
嗨，《办公室女士》。

200
00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:31.580
I'm so glad to be here and to see your beautiful faces and the interior of your closets.
我很高兴能来到这里，见到你们美丽的脸庞，还有你们衣橱的内部。

201
00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:33.780
Yeah, I was going to say, welcome to our closets.
没错，我正想说，欢迎来到我们的衣橱。

202
00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:37.760
Amy, it's so good to see you.
艾米，见到你真好。

203
00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:39.500
Holly Flax.
霍莉·弗拉克斯。

204
00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:41.140
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

205
00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:41.900
Hollis.
霍莉丝。

206
00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:43.720
We found out your name is Hollis.
我们发现你的名字是霍莉丝。

207
00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:44.280
Is that right?
是这样吗？

208
00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:45.780
Because it's on your little name placard.
因为你的小名牌上写着呢。

209
00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:46.220
Yes.
是的。

210
00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:47.740
I don't know if I ever knew that.
我不知道我以前知不知道这一点。

211
00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:49.560
That's a really good tip.
这真是个好建议。

212
00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:52.040
Would you have played the character differently?
你会用不同的方式演绎这个角色吗？

213
00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:53.240
Totally.
绝对会。

214
00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.280
I would have been much more, you know, refined.
我会表现得更，你知道的，文雅一些。

215
00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:59.840
I feel like Hollis is a much more refined.
我觉得霍莉丝这个名字听起来文雅得多。

216
00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:01.400
And maybe I would have had an accent.
也许我还会带点口音。

217
00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:03.480
I don't know.
我不知道。

218
00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:08.360
I think if your character had been a Supreme Court judge, you might have gone by Hollis.
我想如果你的角色是最高法院法官，你可能会用霍莉丝这个名字。

219
00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:09.240
Yes.
是的。

220
00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:14.580
But you ended up in HR, and so you went with the less formal Holly, maybe.
但你最终进了人力资源部，所以可能就用了更随意的霍莉这个名字。

221
00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:15.960
That's correct.
没错。

222
00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:16.660
Yeah.
是啊。

223
00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:22.580
When you run HR, you really want to be more approachable, you know, kind of a, you know,
当你负责人力资源时，你确实想让人觉得更平易近人，你知道，就是那种，你知道的，

224
00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:24.060
warm, fuzzy character.
温暖、亲切的角色。

225
00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:25.740
That makes sense.
这说得通。

226
00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:29.340
Holly was a warm, fuzzy character, and we're in love with her.
霍莉是个温暖、亲切的角色，我们都爱死她了。

227
00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:32.520
I am so enamored by Holly and Michael.
我非常迷恋霍莉和迈克尔。

228
00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:37.800
Jenna and I have cried because Michael is, like, finally with someone that cares about him.
珍娜和我甚至都看哭了，因为迈克尔终于和那个在乎他的人在一起了。

229
00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:39.560
She does.
她确实在乎。

230
00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:49.240
Well, in watching it episode by episode and just four years of Michael not having a partner
嗯，在逐集观看的过程中，看着迈克尔四年都没有伴侣，

231
00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:55.340
to suddenly see a woman partner with him and joke with him and like his jokes, I did.
突然看到一个女人和他成为伴侣，和他开玩笑，还喜欢他的笑话，我确实哭了。

232
00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:55.880
I cried.
我哭了。

233
00:09:56.740 --> 00:10:04.000
Oh, I, you know, in preparation for meeting you both today, I rewatched the episode.
哦，你知道，为了今天能见到你们俩，我重温了那集。

234
00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:06.220
I hadn't watched the show in a long time.
我已经很久没看这部剧了。

235
00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:10.060
I saved her, like, popping in the room when my daughter was on her third pass of the series.
我女儿看第三遍的时候，我刚好看到她闯进房间的那一幕。

236
00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:12.820
But it is so moving.
但这真的很感人。

237
00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:14.620
I think all the characters are so moving.
我觉得所有的角色都很感人。

238
00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.860
There's, I know everyone's talked about this before, but that element of the family,
我知道大家以前都讨论过这个，但那种家庭的元素，

239
00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:19.700
you know, it's not a workplace.
你知道，那不是一个工作场所。

240
00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:20.940
It's actually a family.
那实际上是一个家庭。

241
00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.980
And that really comes through so beautifully.
这一点真的表现得非常美妙。

242
00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:29.280
But Holly and Michael, I mean, I've always said, you know, there's a lid for every pot.
但关于霍莉和迈克尔，我是说，我一直都说，你知道，锅配锅，盖配盖。

243
00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:32.180
So they finally found each other.
所以他们终于找到了彼此。

244
00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:39.820
But she has a high tolerance, I suppose, for that kind of guy.
但我想，她对那种类型的男人有很高的容忍度。

245
00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:45.180
Well, we always like to ask people, Amy, how did you come to be on the show?
嗯，我们总是喜欢问嘉宾，艾米，你是怎么加入这部剧的？

246
00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.200
And we did a little research.
我们做了一点调查。

247
00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:49.680
Paul Lieberstein told us that they came to you.
保罗·李伯斯坦告诉我们是他们主动找的你。

248
00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:55.960
They were huge fans, but that you had simultaneously mentioned to your agent, you might like to do our show.
他们是你的超级粉丝，但你同时也向你的经纪人提过，你可能想演我们的剧。

249
00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:56.760
Yes.
是的。

250
00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:58.300
So what was that process?
那么那个过程是怎样的呢？

251
00:10:58.560 --> 00:10:58.700
Yeah.
是啊。

252
00:10:58.800 --> 00:10:59.800
How did it go down?
当时是怎么回事？

253
00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:01.340
That is true.
确实如此。

254
00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.820
And the timing was eerie because I did say that to my agent.
而且时间点巧得离奇，因为我确实跟我的经纪人说过那话。

255
00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:08.540
It was soon after the Oscars.
那是在奥斯卡颁奖礼之后不久。

256
00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:11.700
And I was like, you know, I'm doing a lot of dramas.
我当时就说，你知道，我一直在演很多正剧。

257
00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:18.060
And if I have any kind of cachet right now at this moment, I want to do a comedy and I want to do a comedy like The Office.
如果我现在还有什么号召力的话，我想演一部喜剧，而且是像《办公室》那样的喜剧。

258
00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:22.800
Because I was never good at the four-camera sitcom.
因为我一直不擅长演那种四机位的情景喜剧。

259
00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:24.720
I just felt too much pressure.
我总觉得压力太大。

260
00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:29.580
And it wasn't like, I always feel like characters weren't really rooted in reality the way The Office was.
而且我觉得那些角色不像《办公室》那样扎根于现实。

261
00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:32.300
And that goes back to how I met Paul Lieberstein.
这就要说到我是怎么认识保罗·利伯斯坦的了。

262
00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:35.300
We were both employed on The Naked Truth.
我们当时都在《赤裸真相》剧组工作。

263
00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:37.600
And he was a writer.
他是编剧。

264
00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:40.160
My agent had kind of reached out.
我的经纪人当时联系过他。

265
00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:43.280
And at the same time, Paul had reached out, I guess, to him.
与此同时，我想保罗也联系了我的经纪人。

266
00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:47.460
I don't know who got there first, but it was, I really think it was like about the same week.
我不知道是谁先联系的，但我真的觉得大概就在同一周。

267
00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:54.040
And Paul had mentioned that he was also a fan of The Wire, as were some of the other writers.
保罗提到过他也是《火线》的剧迷，其他一些编剧也是。

268
00:11:54.260 --> 00:12:01.560
So it was kind of a culmination of all those worlds coming together.
所以这算是所有这些世界交汇在一起的结果。

269
00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:03.820
And did they pitch the character of Holly to you?
他们当时是向你推介霍莉这个角色吗？

270
00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.740
Or did you like have a script to read or anything?
还是说你有剧本可以看之类的？

271
00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:08.460
I don't remember reading a script.
我不记得看过剧本。

272
00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:21.020
I remember saying, hearing it was a one episode only and potential love interest, which obviously I knew was code for, we'll see how you do, kid, if we have you back.
我记得听说这只是个单集角色，而且是潜在的恋爱对象，我当然知道这其实是潜台词，意思是：小姑娘，看你表现如何，再决定要不要让你回来。

273
00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:28.960
I remember also, because I was a big fan of the British series, David finally gets a woman who likes him too.
我还记得，因为我是英版《办公室》的忠实粉丝，大卫终于遇到了一个也喜欢他的女人。

274
00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:32.960
So it was loosely based, I think, on her for the first episode.
所以我认为第一集的内容大致是以她为原型的。

275
00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:35.700
And then our group took it to a different level.
然后我们的团队把它提升到了一个新的高度。

276
00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:41.580
So there was no script that I was privy to, but I knew the kind of general idea of it.
所以我没看过什么剧本，但我知道大概的构思。

277
00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:45.040
What was it like to walk onto the set for the first time?
第一次走进片场是什么感觉？

278
00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:46.820
Really weird.
真的很奇怪。

279
00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:49.620
It's really weird.
真的很奇怪。

280
00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.960
It's because also then, now I'm a fan of the American version.
因为那时我已经成了美版《办公室》的粉丝了。

281
00:12:52.960 --> 00:13:01.000
And you start to know the set, obviously, from the comfort of your couch or your tablet or whatever it is.
你显然已经从沙发、平板电脑或者其他设备上熟悉了那个片场。

282
00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:03.700
You know, when you get there, it's like, oh, everything's smaller.
你知道，当你真正到了那里，你会觉得，噢，一切都变小了。

283
00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:07.480
And then there you all were so warm and welcoming.
然后你们所有人都表现得那么热情好客。

284
00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:10.700
You know, there's always an element of being the new kid in school.
你知道，总有一种转校生的感觉。

285
00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:12.320
And how's that going to go down?
不知道会发生什么？

286
00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:18.760
But, and also you guys at this point are on a really massive, you know, big hit show.
而且你们当时已经是在一部非常火、非常成功的剧组里了。

287
00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:28.820
And it was the most down-to-earth, welcoming, warm group of people, like true ensemble that I met.
但那是我见过最脚踏实地、最热情好客、最温暖的团队，一个真正的集体。

288
00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:34.720
And even to the end, I feel like, you know, you hear all those stories of shows that become so successful.
即使到了最后，我觉得，你知道，你总会听到那些剧集大获成功后的传闻。

289
00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:37.100
And the bad behavior starts coming out.
然后各种恶劣的行为就开始出现了。

290
00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:40.420
And, you know, but at least I didn't see any of that.
但是，至少我没看到任何那种情况。

291
00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:42.840
And I just thought it was always about the work.
我一直觉得大家都是为了工作。

292
00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:46.780
And you guys really set such a beautiful tone there.
你们真的在那里营造了一种非常美好的氛围。

293
00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:49.100
Well, you said you rewatched Business Ethics.
嗯，你说你重温了《商业道德》那一集。

294
00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:56.620
And one of my favorite parts is when you and Michael do the Let's Get Physical.
我最喜欢的部分之一就是你和迈克尔一起跳《Let's Get Physical》那段。

295
00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:58.260
Yes, that's so funny.
是的，那太搞笑了。

296
00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:00.140
I watched that with my daughter.
我和我女儿一起看了。

297
00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:02.740
And we watched that scene like two times in a row.
我们把那个场景连着看了两遍。

298
00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.080
Just him reaching in, trying to hit the play button.
就是他伸手去按播放键的那段。

299
00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:09.560
And then the two of you running in with those headbands and your dance number.
还有你们两个戴着发带跑进来跳舞的那段。

300
00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:11.680
How did you guys come up with all that?
你们是怎么想出这些点子的？

301
00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:12.400
What do we do?
我们该怎么做？

302
00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:15.160
Well, the headbands, I have to give credit for wardrobe.
嗯，关于发带，我得给服装组记上一功。

303
00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:18.540
I mean, that was pretty wonderful and ridiculous.
我是说，那看起来既精彩又荒唐。

304
00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:20.460
Just that having that visual.
光是那种视觉效果就很有趣。

305
00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:24.120
The dance, honestly, that's me just following Steve.
说实话，那段舞蹈我只是跟着史蒂夫跳而已。

306
00:14:25.940 --> 00:14:32.360
Anything, you know, I always, anything that I was lucky enough to be partnered, paired with Steve,
任何时候，你知道，我总是，任何我有幸能和史蒂夫搭档的时候，

307
00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:37.560
Steve, I was smart enough to get in the sidecar of that motorcycle ride.
史蒂夫，我足够聪明，知道要坐上他那辆摩托车的边斗。

308
00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:39.540
Like, I'm not going to leave this here.
就像是，我可不想错过这个机会。

309
00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:41.100
He's going to have a better idea.
他总会有更好的点子。

310
00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:48.460
So that was, that was just me kind of, you know, follow, yeah, literally following his choreography.
所以那其实就是我，你知道，跟着，没错，字面意义上跟着他的编舞走。

311
00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.820
Well, I wasn't there because my character was at art school.
嗯，我当时不在场，因为我的角色在艺术学校。

312
00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:56.780
So I am super bummed that I did not get to see that done live.
所以我超级遗憾没能亲眼看到现场拍摄。

313
00:14:57.560 --> 00:14:59.720
Did you guys do like tons of takes of it?
你们当时拍了很多遍吗？

314
00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:00.760
You were there, Anne.
你在现场的，安妮。

315
00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:01.560
Do you guys remember?
你们还记得吗？

316
00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:03.700
It was so hard not to laugh.
忍住不笑真的太难了。

317
00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:09.380
Like, from the minute it started, and when I was re-watching it, Oscar's face says it all.
从开始的那一刻起，当我重看的时候，奥斯卡的表情说明了一切。

318
00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:10.280
He's in the front.
他站在最前面。

319
00:15:10.360 --> 00:15:11.680
And I was like, I don't want to be in the front.
当时我就想，我可不想站在前面。

320
00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:13.020
Please don't put me in the front.
求求别把我放在前面。

321
00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:15.640
Because I knew I was going to get so tickled.
因为我知道我肯定会忍不住笑场。

322
00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:18.460
And Oscar does this thing when he's trying not to break.
奥斯卡在努力不笑场的时候会做一个动作。

323
00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:22.980
You know, we know each other's tales after all those years where he kind of does this mouth open thing.
你知道，这么多年了，我们都很了解对方的习惯，他会做出那种张着嘴的表情。

324
00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:25.420
And that's him trying not to laugh.
那就是他在努力忍笑。

325
00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:27.020
So you go back and watch.
所以你们回去看看就知道了。

326
00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:28.520
Oscar's like, I'm going to.
奥斯卡当时就像是，我要……

327
00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:31.460
It was so funny.
那真的太搞笑了。

328
00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:37.760
First of all, being in that conference, you know, that's kind of the most famous part of the set, right?
首先，在那间会议室里，你知道，那是片场最出名的地方，对吧？

329
00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:38.540
And here we go.
我们要开始了。

330
00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:39.980
And someone gets to present something.
然后有人要上去做展示。

331
00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:44.060
And it's better when, again, you're paired with someone next to you.
而且当你身边有搭档的时候，效果会更好。

332
00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:49.320
But those scenes where I'm alone giving the conference, my hands are like white knuckled.
但那些我独自做会议展示的场景，我的手都紧张得发白了。

333
00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:52.520
I'm like trying to be like, you know, very casual holding my hands.
我试图表现得，你知道，很随意地放着手。

334
00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:57.340
But if you look closely, my hands are just like two iron fists of nerves.
但如果你仔细看，我的手就像两个因为紧张而握紧的铁拳。

335
00:15:57.780 --> 00:15:58.380
That's the other thing.
还有另一件事。

336
00:15:58.520 --> 00:16:04.300
Like the, everyone always asks me, is the office, was that a lot of improv?
就像，每个人总是问我，《办公室》里有很多即兴表演吗？

337
00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:15.660
And I said, no, it was, I remember scripty coming up to me, you know, correcting me for like of instead of at, like something really tiny.
我说，没有，我记得场记走过来，纠正我把“at”说成了“of”，就那种非常细微的错误。

338
00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:19.260
And I tell people, and tell me if I'm wrong.
我告诉人们，如果我说错了你们纠正我。

339
00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:21.280
I was like, it was heavily scripted.
我说，剧本写得非常详细。

340
00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:28.160
And then maybe by the third take, Steve would go off script first and then everyone would follow suit.
然后可能到了第三遍拍摄时，史蒂夫会先脱稿，然后大家才会跟着即兴发挥。

341
00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:29.840
That's my recollection of it.
这就是我的记忆。

342
00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:31.780
Would you say that's right?
你觉得对吗？

343
00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:32.840
Absolutely.
绝对正确。

344
00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:46.480
Yeah, and then, and in that, I just think it was, again, like, you guys are so in tune with each other and no one trying to beat anyone to the punchline.
没错，而且我觉得，再次强调，你们之间配合得太默契了，没有人试图抢对方的梗。

345
00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:48.280
It was just a constant setup.
那简直就是一场持续的铺垫。

346
00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:51.040
And it was, I thought it was just so perfect.
我觉得那简直太完美了。

347
00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:56.320
And I think that's why people can't really tell between the scripted and when it goes to improv.
我想这就是为什么人们真的分不清哪些是剧本，哪些是即兴发挥。

348
00:16:57.500 --> 00:17:00.920
It was just a, just so perfectly done.
那真的，做得太完美了。

349
00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:04.840
I wanted to ask you about those restaurant scenes.
我想问问你关于那些餐厅场景的事。

350
00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:06.260
Oh, yeah.
噢，没错。

351
00:17:06.860 --> 00:17:07.880
Oh, yeah.
噢，没错。

352
00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:09.140
Cooper's?
库珀的？

353
00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:09.760
Cooper's.
库珀的。

354
00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:15.760
There's that whole runner about the chastity belt where he's trying to explain what a chastity belt is.
有个关于贞操带的梗，他一直在试图解释什么是贞操带。

355
00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:16.340
Oh, my God.
天哪。

356
00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:18.540
And you're like, a chastity belt?
你当时心想，贞操带？

357
00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:27.440
I think, like, the really fun part of their relationship is that it's always a surprise when, like, Holly's on board with him with some joke.
我觉得他们关系中特别有趣的一点是，当霍莉配合他开玩笑时，总是让人感到惊喜。

358
00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:32.080
And then those moments like the chastity belt where she's so horrified.
然后就像贞操带那段一样，她又表现得那么震惊。

359
00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:32.130
Right.
没错。

360
00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:34.300
And yet, so forgiving.
但尽管如此，她还是那么宽容。

361
00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:40.780
You know, here she is, this, you know, she's still flirty with this guy and she likes him and not sure where it's going to go.
你知道，她当时就是这样，她还在和这个男人调情，她喜欢他，也不确定这段关系会走向何方。

362
00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:43.180
But she's going to give that a pass.
但她还是选择了包容。

363
00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:48.040
You know, always with Steve, it was so hard not to laugh.
你知道，和史蒂夫在一起时，总是很难忍住不笑。

364
00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:51.800
It was also like a thrill that sets in.
那也像是一种随之而来的兴奋感。

365
00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:59.480
And look, I remember when I was 25, I went skydiving and I did it where you're strapped to the other instructor.
你看，我记得我25岁的时候去跳伞，是那种绑在教练身上的双人跳伞。

366
00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:06.580
And just as the airplane door opened up and he made me look out, we were about, I guess, 13,000 feet in the air.
就在飞机舱门打开，他让我往外看的时候，我想我们当时大概在13000英尺的高空。

367
00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:12.600
And I remember the thought in my head is, OK, I'm going to die or I'm going to have the time of my life.
我记得当时脑子里闪过的念头是：好吧，要么我会死，要么我会度过人生中最棒的时光。

368
00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:13.380
Pick one.
二选一。

369
00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:15.180
And I remember picking.
我记得我做了选择。

370
00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:16.340
I'm going to have fun.
我要玩得开心。

371
00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:26.260
And as nervous as I was, you know, being like in those scenes with Steve, I remember that moment too.
尽管当时我很紧张，你知道，和史蒂夫演那些戏的时候，我也记得那种感觉。

372
00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:27.580
Like, oh, just pick one.
就像是，噢，选一个吧。

373
00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:28.900
Why don't you have fun instead?
为什么不选择玩得开心呢？

374
00:18:28.900 --> 00:18:35.300
You know, and certainly with him at the lead, like we're all going to look good.
你知道，有他在前面带头，我们肯定都会表现得很好。

375
00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:39.120
You know, he's going to take us along for the ride, I feel.
我觉得他会带着我们一起前进。

376
00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:42.120
But it was so hard not to laugh.
但真的很难忍住不笑。

377
00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:45.680
Like painfully hard not to laugh.
那种忍笑忍到痛苦的感觉。

378
00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:56.700
But I felt like we were dropped into another place of concentration that kind of pulled it all along, if that makes sense.
但我感觉我们进入了一种专注的状态，把一切都串联了起来，如果这说得通的话。

379
00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:57.760
Yeah.
是的。

380
00:18:57.880 --> 00:18:59.320
Did you know Steve before?
你之前认识史蒂夫吗？

381
00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:00.800
Yeah.
认识。

382
00:19:00.980 --> 00:19:06.520
In a very, I had this minuscule part in Dan in Real Life that Steve starred in.
在史蒂夫主演的《冒牌天神2》里，我演了一个非常小的角色。

383
00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:09.900
It was this big extended family comedy.
那是一部关于大家庭的喜剧。

384
00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:13.620
We all, there's a whole group of us there on location in Rhode Island.
我们当时一大群人在罗德岛外景地拍摄。

385
00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:15.060
And so we all became friends.
所以我们都成了朋友。

386
00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:20.760
And then I think it was about like six months after that film is when The Office came up.
我想大概在那部电影拍完六个月后，《办公室》就出现了。

387
00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:24.880
And I believe they ran the idea by Steve of casting me.
我相信他们当时向史蒂夫提议了让我出演的想法。

388
00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:26.140
And I guess it helped.
我想这应该有帮助。

389
00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:29.460
I knew him from Rhode Island for that little bit.
因为在罗德岛那段时间，我认识了他。

390
00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:34.240
Well, we always ask everyone if they have any like favorite memories from their time on the show,
我们总是问每个人，他们在剧组时有没有什么最喜欢的记忆，

391
00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:39.960
like any kind of behind the scenes moment or just something that you remember that stood out to you during your time on The Office.
比如任何幕后花絮，或者你在《办公室》期间记忆深刻、脱颖而出的事情。

392
00:19:40.620 --> 00:19:42.720
Oh my gosh, there's so, so much.
天哪，太多太多了。

393
00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:50.060
I mean, just in a general note, I would honestly, no exaggeration,
我是说，总的来说，我真的，毫不夸张地说，

394
00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:58.180
I would go home feeling like I worked out a series of sit-ups because my stomach muscles were always in play for laughing so hard.
我回家时感觉就像做了一整套仰卧起坐，因为我的腹肌总是因为笑得太厉害而处于紧绷状态。

395
00:19:58.180 --> 00:20:00.960
And I'd just go home exhausted but happy.
我回家时总是筋疲力尽，但很开心。

396
00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:02.820
And that was a nice feeling.
那是一种很棒的感觉。

397
00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:14.860
But there's, and speaking of that, like kind of the uncontrollable laughter, there's the episode where Daryl helps me move and he comes in the car ride, the truck ride.
说到这个，那种无法控制的笑，还有一集是达里尔帮我搬家，他坐进车里，那段卡车之旅。

398
00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:15.460
Yes.
是的。

399
00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:15.780
Yeah.
没错。

400
00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:32.120
Me, Steve and Craig, like holed up in that truck all day, kind of, probably we had a little bit of lack of oxygen, but we couldn't, we just,
我和史蒂夫还有克雷格，整天被困在那辆卡车里，可能我们当时有点缺氧，但我们就是忍不住，我们只能……

401
00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:35.320
we were in hysterics and could barely get through the scenes.
我们当时笑得前仰后合，几乎没法顺利演完那些戏份。

402
00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:47.300
Um, and it was, that, that was like one of the highlights of, of my time there was just spending the day with those two guys, um, just laughing.
嗯，那真的是我在那儿度过的一段最美好的时光，就是和那两个家伙待了一整天，嗯，一直在笑。

403
00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:53.400
I mean, I say that, but I'd also be on set and laughing all the time too.
我是说，虽然我这么说，但我在片场时也总是笑个不停。

404
00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:55.740
It was just very new to me.
这对我来说真的很新鲜。

405
00:20:56.120 --> 00:20:59.380
I usually end up in these dramas where I'm crying all day.
我通常接的都是那种整天都在哭的剧。

406
00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:03.140
So this is a much better way to spend your day.
所以这样度过一天要好得多。

407
00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:04.500
I remember that too.
我也记得那件事。

408
00:21:04.680 --> 00:21:09.880
And I remember my agents asking me if I wanted to do dramas in my downtime.
我还记得我的经纪人问我，在休息期间想不想接点剧。

409
00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:12.100
You mentioned, you were like, I've done a lot of dramas.
你提到了，你说，我已经演过很多剧了。

410
00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:12.920
I want to do a comedy.
我想演喜剧。

411
00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:15.200
They were like, do you want to take a stab at some drama?
他们问，你想尝试一下正剧吗？

412
00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:17.980
And I was like, I don't think so.
我说，我想还是算了吧。

413
00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:24.300
I think I'm really liking the jobs where I just laugh all day because I'm like in a good mood a lot.
我觉得我真的很喜欢那些能让我整天大笑的工作，因为这样我大部分时间心情都很好。

414
00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:27.460
So let's just, let's keep doing the comedies.
所以我们就继续演喜剧吧。

415
00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:32.360
It was, it was like, um, it was a happy place to work.
那真的是一个，嗯，一个让人感到快乐的工作场所。

416
00:21:33.140 --> 00:21:36.100
My only request was if I could ever have a scene where I didn't frown.
我唯一的请求就是，能不能给我安排一场不用皱眉头的戏。

417
00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:38.060
That's true.
确实如此。

418
00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:40.080
You had to frown your way through this comedy.
你在这部喜剧里全程都得皱着眉头。

419
00:21:42.080 --> 00:21:44.680
Well, do you get recognized as Holly Flax?
那么，大家会认出你是霍莉·弗莱克斯吗？

420
00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:45.960
Like when you're out and about?
比如当你出门在外的时候？

421
00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:47.860
Oh, I do.
哦，会的。

422
00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:48.740
I do.
会的。

423
00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:49.720
And it's interesting.
这很有趣。

424
00:21:50.040 --> 00:22:02.300
Uh, I'm sure, I'm sure you guys have noticed that there's being, uh, being recognized when the show was on the air and then the whole streaming, um,
呃，我相信，我相信你们也注意到了，在剧集播出时被认出来，和后来整个流媒体，嗯，

425
00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:07.120
version of the show that has kind of taken it to a whole new level.
剧集的版本，把这种知名度提升到了一个全新的水平。

426
00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:11.940
And everyone's getting younger, my daughter's friends.
而且大家越来越年轻了，我女儿的朋友们也是。

427
00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:24.880
And, um, um, so yeah, I do, I still get, I still get recognized, you know, years ago, not that long ago, a few years ago, my family and our really good friends.
而且，嗯，嗯，所以是的，我确实，我还是会，我还是会被认出来，你知道，几年前，没过多久，几年前，我和我的家人还有我们非常好的朋友。

428
00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:32.260
And we went to Scranton because there's this amazing, um, antique store called all good things.
我们去了斯克兰顿，因为那里有一家很棒的，嗯，古董店，叫“万事皆好”。

429
00:22:32.300 --> 00:22:40.580
And it's just a warehouse, a giant warehouse of just every old antique knob, hinge, dresser, door, toilet.
那其实就是一个仓库，一个巨大的仓库，里面堆满了各种旧古董，旋钮、铰链、梳妆台、门、马桶。

430
00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:41.400
Sign me up, Amy.
算我一个，艾米。

431
00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:42.720
It goes on, it goes on forever.
东西多得没完没了。

432
00:22:42.940 --> 00:22:43.580
It's really cool.
真的很酷。

433
00:22:43.880 --> 00:22:52.440
And so we, we, we go there and then we're looking for something to eat and we're hungry and they pull in and I was kind of behind the eight ball on this.
所以我们，我们，我们去了那里，然后我们想找点吃的，我们饿了，他们把车开了进去，我当时有点措手不及。

434
